# The Mole Concept: Counting Particles by Weighing

> Atoms are too small to count one by one, so chemists count in
> packages of 6.022 × 10²³ — the mole — converting freely between
> mass, particles, and gas volume.

## 1. Why a Counting Unit?

A single drop of water holds more molecules than there are stars
visible in the sky. Chemistry needs a bridge between the atomic
scale (particles) and the laboratory scale (grams) — and the mole
is that bridge.

## 2. The Mole and Avogadro's Number

[definition]
A **mole** is the amount of substance containing as many particles
as there are atoms in exactly 12 g of carbon-12. That count is
**Avogadro's number**: 6.022 × 10²³ particles per mole.
[/definition]

@diagram tree id="mole-map" layout="tree" aria="The mole connects mass, particle count and gas volume"
  "Moles (n)" -> "Mass: n times molar mass M"
  "Moles (n)" -> "Particles: n times 6.022 x 10^23"
  "Moles (n)" -> "Gas at STP: n times 22.4 L"
@/diagram

## 3. The Master Formulas

@math label="eq:mole-relations"
n = \frac{m}{M} = \frac{N}{N_A} = \frac{V_{\mathrm{gas}}}{22.4\ \mathrm{L}}
@/math

@math label="eq:molarity"
\text{Molarity} = \frac{n_{\mathrm{solute}}}{V_{\mathrm{solution\ (L)}}}
@/math

STP means 273 K (0 °C) and 1 atm — the conditions at which one mole
of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L.

## 4. Worked Examples

[example]
**Mass to particles.** 18 g of water: n = 18/18 = 1 mol, so
6.022 × 10²³ molecules. With 3 atoms each, that is about
1.8 × 10²⁴ atoms.
[/example]

[example]
**A second mass.** 44 g of CO₂: n = 44/44 = 1 mol, again
6.022 × 10²³ molecules.
[/example]

[example]
**Gas volume.** 11.2 L of any gas at STP: n = 11.2/22.4 = 0.5 mol,
so 3.011 × 10²³ molecules.
[/example]

[example]
**Molarity.** 5.85 g of NaCl (M = 58.5 g/mol) in 500 mL of
solution: n = 5.85/58.5 = 0.1 mol, and molarity = 0.1/0.500
= 0.2 mol/L.
[/example]

## 5. Percentage Composition

| Compound | Calculation | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| H in H₂O | 2(1)/18 | ≈ 11.1% |
| O in H₂O | 16/18 | ≈ 88.9% |
| C in CO₂ | 12/44 | ≈ 27.3% |

## 6. Empirical and Molecular Formula

[definition]
The **empirical formula** is the simplest whole-number ratio of
atoms; the **molecular formula** is the actual count — always a
whole-number multiple of the empirical.
[/definition]

A compound found to be 40.0% C, 6.7% H, 53.3% O divides by atomic
masses to the ratio 3.33 : 6.7 : 3.33, which simplifies to
1 : 2 : 1 — empirical formula CH₂O. If the molar mass is 180, the
molecular formula is (CH₂O)₆ = C₆H₁₂O₆ — glucose.

## 7. Equations as Mole Bookkeeping

Read 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O as "2 mol of hydrogen react with 1 mol of
oxygen to give 2 mol of water":

@math label="eq:mass-conservation"
2(2\ \mathrm{g}) + 32\ \mathrm{g} \longrightarrow 2(18\ \mathrm{g})
@/math

So 4 g + 32 g → 36 g — mass is conserved because atoms are merely
rearranged.

[info title="Limiting reagent"]
The **limiting reagent** is the reactant that runs out first and
caps the yield. Mix 10 g of H₂ with 32 g of O₂: the 5 mol of H₂
would need 2.5 mol of O₂ but only 1 mol is present, so O₂ is
limiting and at most 36 g of water forms.
[/info]

## 8. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Avogadro's number? | 6.022 × 10²³ per mole |
| Molar volume at STP? | 22.4 L per mole |
| Moles in 18 g of H₂O? | 1 mol |
| Empirical formula of glucose? | CH₂O |
| Why is mass conserved? | atoms are only rearranged |
